  "step_2": "The individual compares the two lotteries by noting that Lottery B has only 1% less chance of winning than Lottery A (33% vs 34%), but offers 100€ more (2500 vs 2400). The individual finds it reasonable to give up 1% winning probability in favor of the higher monetary amount. The justification centers on a trade-off between a small decrease in probability and a gain in outcome value. The individual focuses on the magnitude of the prize and the marginal difference in winning chances, indicating a preference for higher payoff despite slightly lower odds. No mention is made of an aspiration level, average outcomes, or evaluating outcomes based on whether they exceed a reference point derived from all possible outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing an aspiration level from the mean of all outcomes and then comparing the probabilities that each lottery yields outcomes above this level. The individual does consider the outcomes and probabilities, but not in the way prescribed by the reason. Instead of calculating a reference point (aspiration level) and assessing which lottery more frequently exceeds it, the individual performs a direct trade-off between the 1% lower chance and the 100€ higher reward. There is no indication that the individual computed an average of all outcomes or used such a value as a benchmark for evaluating gains or losses. The justification is based on intuitive compensation (more money for slightly lower odds), not on the structure of the aspiration-based rule. Therefore, the reasoning process described does not align with the decision reason in terms of either information used or decision rule applied.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

{
