  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes and then comparing the probabilities of exceeding this level. The individual does not mention or imply any such calculation. They do not refer to an average outcome, nor do they evaluate which lottery gives a better chance of achieving outcomes above a computed reference point. Instead, their reasoning is based on the small probability of a catastrophic loss and a subjective belief in personal luck. This is conceptually different from the aspiration-level rule. There is no evidence that the individual computed outcome differences from a mean or evaluated the frequency of positive deviations. Therefore, the information and rule central to the decision reason are absent from the verbal report. The individual’s justification is based on probability neglect of rare events and optimism bias, not on aspiration-level reasoning.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

{
